### .NET Version

Client: `@microsoft/signalr` 6.0.25 to 11.0.0 (Node.js). Server: ASP.NET Core SignalR behind a reverse proxy.

### Anything else?

Workaround: a watchdog that drops the `HubConnection` (without awaiting `stop()`) and builds a new one when the connection is not `Connected` for too long. Forcing the WebSockets transport is not enough (see `repro-ws.js`).

Possible fixes: in `_stopConnection`, do not leave the `HttpConnection` half-stopped when it is called in the `Connecting` state (e.g. fail the pending start instead of throwing from the transport callback), and/or make `_stopInternal` resolve `_stopPromise` when there is no transport.

